      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "We hypothesized that orthostatic intolerance (OI) is associated with gastric dysrhythmias, nausea, and abdominal pain, which improves using fludrocortisone to treat OI."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Patients (n=16, girls) with OI completed questionnaires before and after fludrocortisone treatment (age 14.8 ± 2.8 years). Ten patients underwent electrogastrograms (EGGs) before fludrocortisone."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "All EGGs showed gastric dysrhythmias. Fludrocortisone reduced mean scores as follows: nausea, 3.1 ± 0.8 to 2.1 ± 1.1 (P=0.016); dizziness, 3.0 ± 1.0 to 2.2 ± 1.1 (P=0.0371); abdominal pain, 2.8 ± 1.3 to 1.9 ± 1.4 (P=0.0063); flushing, 2.3 ± 1.2 to 1.5 ± 1.4 (P=0.0476); and missing school, 2.2 ± 1.5 to 1.2 ± 1.5 (P=0.0078)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Chronic nausea and abdominal pain accompany OI and improve with OI treatment."
        }
      ]},
